  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nNorthwesterly upper flow Sunday evening is forecast to rapidly \ndecay into zonal flow (west to east) by Monday morning. \nEnsembles show this flow pattern lasting through Tuesday, with a\nfew weak systems embedded in the flow. A shortwave trough looks\nto develop over the region Monday, though 24 hr precipitation \nguidance from both the NBM and LREF are in support of no \nprecipitation with this system. Tuesday, a weak embedded ridge \nis favored to be in place. Overall, mild and dry conditions are \nexpected Sunday night through Tuesday. Highs are forecast in the\n50s, with a 30-40% chance of seeing 60s in some areas, and lows\nin the 20s to mid 30s. \n\nEnsembles show a deeper trough across the west Wednesday \nmorning, lifting northeastward through the remainder of the \nforecast period. A surface low is expected to be favorably \nlocated at the leading edge of this trough, and in the left exit\nregion of a modest 60-75 kt 500-mb jet. Theta-e surfaces show \nthat moisture convergence is likely across the area as the \nsurface low approaches, with a moist Pacific airmass meeting \nmoist gulf air. Precipitation is forecast with this system as it\ncomes through. Around 90% of LREF guidance supports this low \ntracking to the south of the forecast region, suggesting that \nprecipitation along and behind a cold front is the likely \nsolution. NBM guidance suggests that portions of Eastern \nColorado and Southwest Nebraska have a 50% chance or greater of \nlows below freezing with this system, which would support a \nmixed precipitation event. GFS and EC model soundings suggest \nthat rain and snow are the most likely at this time. \n\nThere is a reasonable amount of uncertainty on the timing of \nthis event, as seen in the most recent deterministic guidance \nwith the GFS progressing the system slower than the EC and CMC. \nTurning to ensembles, the trough axis at 500-mb Wednesday \nmorning is consistently over Southern California. However, GEFS,\nEC, and LREF spread is highest on the leading edge of the \nfeature, which would indicate a faster progression, similar to \nthe 00Z deterministic EC and CMC models. Looking at LREF \nmembers, this would put precipitation onset and frontal passage \naround Wednesday evening. However, if a scenario similar to the \nGFS occurs, frontal passage and precipitation onset may not be \nuntil as late as Thursday night. Currently, around 75% of LREF \nmembers show high temperatures below 50 F Thursday, which would \nsuggest the faster solution, with precipitation and cooler \ntemperatures arriving Wednesday evening, is favored. Cooler \ntemperatures would be expected to remain through the end of the \nperiod. As for high temperatures Wednesday ahead of the surface \nlow's arrival, anticipated warm advection alongside NBM and LREF\nprobabilistic guidance suggests mid 50s to lower 60s is \nappropriate.",
